# GritQL in Biome — what actually works

Everything here was verified empirically against **Biome 2.5.8** by running `biome lint` against
real fixtures. Biome's GritQL engine is not the full Grit language and its documentation does not
enumerate the differences, so this file is the working reference for anyone editing these rules.

> **An earlier revision of this file was wrong in three load-bearing ways**, and those errors are
> why several rules were originally built out of large regexes over node text. It claimed node
> kinds were a last resort, that class and interface bodies were unreachable, and that recursive
> pattern definitions always crash. All three are false. If you are reading a rule that matches
> source text with a big `r"..."`, it is probably rewritable on node kinds, and probably has a
> substring bug. See [PARITY.md](./PARITY.md) for which ones were fixed.

## Reach for node kinds first

There are two ways to match. Node-kind patterns are the primary tool; snippets are the
convenience.

```grit
// node kind, with fields bound by name
TsTypeAssertionExpression(ty = $type, expression = $operand) as $node
JsVariableDeclarator(variable_annotation = TsTypeAnnotation(ty = $t)) as $node

// snippet
`Reflect.get($args)` as $node
```

Prefer a node kind whenever the construct has one. Snippets only match what Biome's parser accepts
in that exact position, and matching source text with a regex is worse still: regexes see raw text,
so they match substrings of unrelated constructs, and they are recompiled per candidate node.

**The failure mode of a node kind is safe.** An unknown node kind or field name is a hard
`Failed to compile the Grit plugin` error, never a silent no-match, so a typo cannot quietly turn a
rule into a no-op. A regex that is subtly wrong fails silently forever.

### Verified node kinds

| Construct | Node kind | Note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `x as T` | `TsAsExpression` | |
| `<T>x` | `TsTypeAssertionExpression(ty, expression)` | no snippet reaches this |
| `let` / `var` / `const` declarator | `JsVariableDeclarator(id, variable_annotation, initializer)` | reaches `let`, which no snippet does |
| class property | `JsPropertyClassMember(property_annotation, value)` | |
| class index signature | `TsIndexSignatureClassMember` | inside a class body |
| interface / type-literal index signature | `TsIndexSignatureTypeMember` | inside an interface body |
| mapped type | `TsMappedType` | |
| the `object` keyword | `TsNonPrimitiveType` | |
| object literal | `JsObjectExpression` | **not** an object binding pattern |
| array literal | `JsArrayExpression` | **not** an array binding pattern |
| block statement | `JsBlockStatement` | |
| function body | `JsFunctionBody` | |
| switch clauses | `JsCaseClause`, `JsDefaultClause` | braceless clauses still bind names |
| intersection / union | `TsIntersectionType`, `TsUnionType` | no arity cap, unlike hand-written branches |

Class and interface bodies are reachable **by node kind**. Only snippets cannot see into them.

## Plugin file shape

```grit
language js

`Reflect.get($args)` as $node where {
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $node,
    message = "anti-slop/no-reflect-get: use typed property access",
    severity = "error"
  )
}
```

- `language js` covers `.js`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, `.tsx`.
- `span` must be a **named** metavariable. `span = $_` fails with
  `cannot make resolved pattern from arbitrary pattern UNDERSCORE`.
- `severity` is one of `hint`, `info`, `warn`, `error`.
- These rules never rewrite with `=>`: every one wants a human to make the call.

## Recursion: guarded recursion works

A pattern definition **may** call itself, as long as every recursive branch consumes something so
the recursion cannot re-enter on the same node:

```grit
// verified: peels (((unknown))) and correctly rejects ((string))
pattern peel_unknown() {
  or { `unknown`, `($inner)` where { $inner <: peel_unknown() } }
}
```

What crashes the Biome worker with a stack overflow is *unguarded* self-recursion, where a branch
can re-enter without descending. Guarded recursion is the correct replacement for hand-unrolling a
depth limit; several rules still cap parenthesis depth at two only because this file used to say
recursion was impossible.

`contains X until Y` is the other bounded-walk tool: it walks a subtree but refuses to descend into
nodes matching `Y`.

```grit
// unwrap any depth of parentheses
$operand <: contains $inner until not `($_)`
```

## Regexes: three traps

Regexes are the last resort. Biome uses the Rust `regex` crate, so there is no catastrophic
backtracking and no lookaround or backreferences are available. The traps are elsewhere.

**They match raw source text, so they match substrings of other constructs.** A regex for
`Record<string, unknown>` also matches inside `Partial<Record<string, unknown>>`, which is not the
same type. Anchor them, or use node kinds.

**Full-match anchoring binds looser than alternation.** `r"true|false|null"` parses as
`(^true$)|(^false$)|(^null$)` only if you group it; unguarded, it matched the identifiers
`trueValue`, `maybenull` and `falseAlarm` in a shipped rule. Always group: `r"(?:true|false|null)"`.

**Cost scales with regex source size times node count.** Each `r"..."` is recompiled per candidate
node. Two ~700-character regexes tested against every node cost 2.31s on a 17KB file containing
nothing to report.

## `until` and `within` are tested against the root node too

`contains X until Y` tests `Y` against the subtree root before descending, and `within` walks all
the way up to the module node. A whole-node regex used as a boundary is therefore also tested
against the **entire file's text**, and a file whose own text happens to full-match the boundary
silently disables the rule for that file.

This is not theoretical: a file starting with a generic function and ending in `}` matched a
boundary regex and suppressed every module-level diagnostic in it. Use node kinds for boundaries.

## Dots snippets abort the file when they meet a binding pattern

`` `{ $... }` `` and `` `[$...]` `` look like harmless "any object literal" patterns, but during a
walk they are also tested against object and array *binding* patterns. When one is reached the
engine aborts the entire file:

```
i my-rule errored: Dots should only be directly within a list pattern.
```

The rule then silently reports nothing for that file. Use `JsObjectExpression()`,
`JsArrayExpression()`, `JsBlockStatement()` or `JsFunctionBody()` instead, which are also more
accurate, since a destructuring pattern is not a literal.

This shipped in two rules and was caught only by running them over a real codebase.

## Other confirmed snippet limits

These are limits of **snippets**. Check the node-kind table above before concluding something is
unreachable.

- **Type annotations are not standalone constructs.** `` `$p: unknown` `` matches nothing. Match an
  enclosing declaration and refine with `contains`.
- **Individual typed parameters.** `` `function $n($p: unknown) { $body }` `` does not match
  `function handle(input: unknown) { … }`. Match the whole parameter list, then `contains`.
- **Interface and class bodies.** No snippet form matches them. Node kinds do.
- **`let` bindings.** A metavariable in a `let` binding position never matches.
  `JsVariableDeclarator` does.
- **Type parameter lists.** A metavariable inside `<…>` fails to compile the plugin.
- **Empty function bodies.** `$body` does not bind to `{}`.
- **Bare index-signature type literals.** `` `{ [$k: string]: $v }` `` parses as a block.
- **`contains` stops at its first match**, so a rule that reports from inside a `contains` reports
  once per container. To report every occurrence, register the diagnostic and then force the
  predicate to fail (see the trick at the end of `no-widen-then-assert.grit`).

## No metavariable interpolation in messages

`register_diagnostic`'s `message` does not interpolate: a message containing `$name` emits the
literal characters `$name`. Every diagnostic here is static, where the oxlint originals name the
offending symbol.

## No scope analysis, no types, no dataflow

There is no way to ask whether an identifier resolves to the global binding, to follow a type alias
to its definition, or to relate two statements. Rules that depended on those in oxlint are
approximated, and their limits are recorded in [PARITY.md](./PARITY.md).

## Diagnostics, suppression, and categories

Every plugin diagnostic lands in the single `plugin` category. Consequences:

- Suppress with `// biome-ignore lint: reason` or `// biome-ignore lint/plugin: reason` on the
  preceding line. `// biome-ignore plugin: reason` does **not** work.
- A suppression silences *all* plugin diagnostics on that line, not one rule. This is why every
  message is prefixed `anti-slop/<rule-name>:`.
- Rules cannot be toggled through `linter.rules`. Granularity comes from which `.grit` files are
  listed in `plugins`.

## One bad plugin kills the whole run

If any configured plugin fails to load or compile, Biome emits `Error(s) during loading of
plugins` and lints **nothing** — with `--reporter=json` it produces no JSON at all. A broken plugin
looks exactly like a clean codebase to any script that only checks the exit code.
`test/integration.mjs` and `biome-anti-slop doctor` both exist because of this.

## Testing a rule

`test/harness.mjs` fails on false negatives, false positives, and any location-less plugin
diagnostic, so a green run means the pattern genuinely fires.

That is necessary but not sufficient. Three shipped defects came from fixtures that exercised
constructs only in isolation at top level. Every rule's fixtures must also contain the hostile
constructs in `test/fixtures/_hostile.ts`: a destructuring declaration, a nested block, a `.tsx`
file, a file whose text could full-match a boundary regex, and repeated occurrences. Rules are also
timed by the harness, because the failure mode of an accidentally quadratic pattern is a hang, not
a wrong answer.
